'''buckle'''<sup>2</sup> (buk′l), ''n.'' [< ME. ''bokle, bokel, bocle'', etc., < OF. ''bocle, bucle'', F. ''boucle'', the boss of a shield, a ring, a buckle, = Pr. ''bocla, bloca'' = OSp. ''bloca'' = MLG. ''bokele'' = MD. ''boeckel, bockel'' = MHG. ''buckel'', boss of a shield, G. ''buckel'', a boss, knob, hump, < ML. ''bucula, buccula'', a beaver, a shield, the boss of a shield, a buckle, L. ''buccula'', a beaver, a little cheek or mouth, dim. of ''bucca'', cheek: see ''bucca''.] '''1.''' A clasp consisting of a rectangular or curved rim, with one or more movable tongues secured to the chape at one side or in the middle, and long enough to rest upon the opposite side: used for fastening together two straps or belts or the ends of the same strap, or for some similar purpose. {{smaller|It is sewed or otherwise fastened to one band or end, and the other is passed through it, being kept from slipping by the tongue or tongues. Buckles for use in dress have often been made highly ornamental, especially for shoes. See ''shoe-buckle''.}}

'''2.''' In ''her.'', same as ''arming-buckle''.—'''3.''' An iron loop for fastening the blade to the frame of a wood-saw.—{{smaller|'''To turn the buckle of the belt behind''', to prepare to join in close fight.}}

'''buckle'''<sup>2</sup> (buk′l), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''buckled'', ppr. ''buckling''. [< ME. ''buclen, bokelen, boclen'', buckle, stud, < OF. *''bocler'', ''boucler'', F. ''boucler'', buckle;

readings.c1s2:
from the noun.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To fasten with a buckle or buckles.—'''2.''' To prepare for action of any kind (a metaphor taken from buckling on armor previous to engaging in battle); hence, to set vigorously to work at anything: with a reflexive pronoun.
{{smaller block|The Sarazin . . . ''him buckled'' to the field.
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., I. vi. 41.}}}}
{{smaller block|Hereupon Cartwright ''buckled himself'' to the employment.
{{right|''Fuller.''}}}}
'''3†.''' To join in battle.
{{smaller block|The foot . . . were ''buckled'' with them in front.
{{right|''Sir J. Hayward.''}}}}
'''4.''' To confine or limit. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|<poem>How brief the life of man, . . .
That the stretching of a span
''Buckles'' in his sum of age.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', As you Like it, iii. 2.}}}}
'''5.''' To join together; unite in marriage. [Scotch.]
{{smaller block|Dr. R., who ''buckles'' beggars for a tester and a dram of Geneva.
{{right|''Scott.''}}}}
'''II.†''' ''intrans.'' To marry.
{{smaller block|Good silly Stellio, we must shortly ''buckle.''
{{right|''Mother Bombie.'' (''Halliwell.'')}}}}

'''buckle-beggar''' (buk′l-beg″är), ''n.'' [Sc., also ''buckle-the-beggars;'' < ''buckle''<sup>2</sup>, ''v.'', 5, + obj. ''beggar.''] A person who performs the ceremony of marriage in a clandestine and irregular manner. ''Scott.''

